A||||6||MrPBody||2.5 head bolt torque||||5||
Z||000000||MrPBody||01-26-2001||11:24 PM||||Searches returned some related information but not exactly an answer to this question.<P>My Haynes manual specifies two methods for torquing head bolts on a 2.5:<P>1984-1985: torque to a specified value<P>1986-on: torque to a lower value, then turn an additional <I>x</I> degrees.<P>The 1985 Helm manual also specifies a simple torque value.<P>Which method should I use for my '85 2.5?  I'm guessing that '85 and '86 engines are identical, and that the torque-angle procedure is correct for all years.<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||01-26-2001||11:44 PM||||1. replace ALL the head bolts if you haven't already. They are available thru FelPro and several other sources. TTY bolts are not reusable.<P>2. follow the Torque then angle procedure. WARNING! not all bolts get the same torque or angle.<P>TTY bolts should be used in ALL 4 cyl Fiero motors. The original bolts, even if they were not TTY, are known to be weak and must not be reused.<P>I have got to put this in my cave..... In my spare time...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||moonie||01-27-2001||12:49 AM||||i'm waiting on a head & head bolts to be shipped to me. <BR>i knew the head bolts had to be torqued but what is this angle you refer to ?<BR>i thought i just followed the diagram that comes with the head to torqe the bolts.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-27-2001||03:42 AM||||I have a 1984 sc.  I just finished the head gasket replacement about 1 week ago.  I torqued the head bolts to 92 ft-lbs as recommended by Chilton's.  It was the most recent specification:<BR>1.  Head bolts came with instructions printed in 1996<BR>2.  Haynes was printed in 1989<BR>3.  Chilton's was printed in 1997<BR>Chiltn's made clear that 1984s don't get the angle torque sequence.  The only reason I could deduce was that they meant it.<BR>So, I torqued to 92 ft-lbs on every bolt in the proper sequence.<BR>The compression test I did after 300 miles of driving yeilded 160 psi in all four cylinders.  The gasket is sealed.<BR>I bought my head bolts using "partsamerica.com"  they were quick and they had the parts.  I recommend them. <BR>While replacing the head gasket, I ground the burrs out of the intake and exhaust passages in the head and manifolds. <BR>I "rebuilt" the distributor by replacing the pickup coil and ignition control module.  All the filters (air, crankcase, fuel), pcv vavle, starter, alternator, belts, hoses, throttle position sensor, oxygen sensor, distributor cap, rotor, plugs, and wires were replaced at the same time. Timing is set at 9 degrees BTDC.<BR>My engine no longer hesitates or leaks.  The power is dramatically improved.  And the car sounds a lot better.  I am running 10W40 synthetic oil which makes the lifters a lot quieter.  It took a lot of time and money, but the difference is remarkable . . .<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrPBody||01-27-2001||08:58 AM||||<I>Merci</I>, Ogre.<P>I did get new head bolts, along with a rebuilt head.  My spare head had numerous cracks in the valve seats.  I'll bet the head now on the car is cracked, too.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Motorhead||01-27-2001||08:26 PM||||New head and 40,000 miles later the Hayne's Manual torque and angle specs are holding up thus far! <BR>Heck!  The motor doesn't even leak oil. Much!<BR>New bolts of coarse!<BR>Mike <P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0">  <p>[This message has been edited by Motorhead (edited 01-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-28-2001||03:36 PM||||Keep in mind TTY headbolts are not made the same way as regular bolts. If you replace regular bolts with TTY then you should probably apply the TTY torque rules. <P>It's hard to say just what will happen if you don't. It might hold forever, it might start leaking later. <P>If the a book lists a single torque number for a motor then its a good bet they didn't use TTY in it at that point. Problem is the bolts they did use where weak. You have to replace them either way. The new bolts are all TTY.||||reg||1
